  "analytic_review_groups": [
    "Ideal projection: increasing finite maxima and fixed-stalk strong openness preserve every multiplier ideal; normalized pointwise supremum gives idempotence.",
    "Ordinary projection lies between a translate of the original potential and its ideal envelope; both retain the original ideal type.",
    "Analytic and finite-mass countable divisorial types convert ideal domination to ordinary domination; rational analytic coefficients retain their convention.",
    "Smooth gauge changes move the zero obstacle; bounded comparisons of envelopes do not imply invariant raw Monge-Ampere mass in a nonclosed background.",
    "Infinite-mass good potentials can have finite-mass models; the envelope-relative class is kept separate from unrestricted equation claims.",
    "Ideal-class L1 metric is complete only after ambient completion; atomic curve models approximate smooth nonconstant potentials outside the ideal-model image.",
    "Damped capacity and fixed positive-mass analytic approximations distinguish L1 convergence, capacity convergence, ordinary distance and scalar normalization.",
    "Ordered ideal-envelope convergence needs a common ideal order and all divisorial limits; blowups at every point identify valuation inequalities.",
    "Fixed-weight ideal inclusion with discrepancy error gives divisorial convergence; naked L1 convergence and even all curve valuations do not replace the order hypothesis.",
    "Dyadic subadditivity orders ordinary types rather than representatives; curvature-error backgrounds decrease and rational scaling absorbs the Hermitian error.",
    "Ordinary-class topology uses all fixed modifications and controlled ideals; no countable cofinal family or separation assertion is inferred.",
    "Cutoff sublogarithmic poles show nonseparation of ordinary classes; bounded model mass follows from contact and lower semicontinuity, not a mass bound for the original potential.",
    "Diffuse polar approximation shows incomparable zero-obstacle topologies after smooth gauge changes; all-obstacle coordinates repair transport.",
    "Countably many uniformly dense smooth obstacles suffice by the obstacle Lipschitz estimate; modifications still range over the full fixed family.",
    "Ideal obstacle reprojection preserves ideal type and reduces mixed-obstacle equality to membership in a relative envelope class; the general converse remains unresolved.",
    "Finite unramified covers use local branch maxima and canonical convex stretching inequalities, with exact ideal pullback and no ramification factors.",
    "Connected-fiber divisor reduction allows smooth nonpositive base backgrounds and entire fibers in the divisor; induced obstacles can be infinite.",
    "Extended continuity of the fiber infimum uses compact fiber minima and local sections; a nonempty finite-obstacle base open set gives a uniform bound for all competitors.",
    "Divisorial removal, descent, ideal cancellation and submersion ideal pullback identify full ordinary and ideal competitor families.",
    "Relative normal crossings are required only for the explicit coordinate ideal formula; the ramified graph counterexample does not invalidate envelope reduction.",
    "Top-degree mass vanishes after base descent, using corrected signed local bounded products for truncations; no positivity of the base form is silently imposed.",
    "All-obstacle converse uses truncated divisorial obstacles, valuation convergence, Hartogs maxima and a volume-zero whole-fiber locus; relative stretching descends canonically at poles.",
    "Disconnected fibers require the finite component cover. The elliptic multiplication example rules out the original-base infimum; curve polar equality is reflected by the covering.",
    "Zero-volume divisorial products retain distinct ordinary and ideal projections and nonanalytic types; identical zero volumes give no equality conclusion.",
    "Curve-polar metrics transfer isometrically to closed, nonclosed and degenerating product loci, with atomic nearest-point retraction, rational analytic closure and fiberwise L1 bounds.",
    "Separated curve envelopes are proved by partial supremum and slice induction; atomic Green representatives give the ideal identity without a positive-area assumption in every coordinate.",
    "Separated smooth obstacles admit exact coordinate formulas, while mixed obstacles admit an equality criterion but not a coordinate formula.",
    "Active curve coefficients rescale distance and zero coefficients delete coordinates; uniform normalized L1 bounds control changing potentials even on pole sets of measure zero.",
    "Rational analytic scaling is explicitly tied to the book convention; changing active sets destroys bijective coordinate identification, while ordered coefficients still give surjective contractions.",
    "Bounded noncompact Gromov-Hausdorff estimates use correspondences and diameters; ordered coefficients give exact distances and fiber diameters.",
    "Arbitrary curve backgrounds are compared by area, with smooth zero-area coordinates retained in potentials; closed test forms prove nonbigness and gauge correction proves bigness in the stated regime.",
    "Semipositive auxiliary terms have only auxiliary directions; slice comparison and finite divisor mass work on non-Kahler factors without ambient mass bounds.",
    "The Hopf sixfold has infinite upper mass by positive block products with the factor 120; the zero-volume polar model locus still has diameter four.",
    "Isolated point poles have zero first polar current in dimension at least two; blowup detects their divisor coefficient, and countably many modifications miss a Baire-generic point.",
    "Gauduchon trace normalization gives a bounded comparison and separates analytic models; complete coordinate products do not assert realization by a model.",
    "Closed null-carrier trace recovery, compatible pushforwards and a summable common pole carrier give current limits, without identifying the carrier potential as their realization.",
    "Independent Gauduchon choices on surfaces can suppress moving isolated poles while the exact volume loss remains a squared logarithmic coefficient.",
    "Diffuse Cantor maxima have Hausdorff-zero poles, rank-deficient products off poles and probability truncation mass; the anisotropic simplex determinant fixes the tensor constant exactly.",
    "Diffuse cutoffs lose only top-degree mass; Hermitian representative polynomials retain background derivative terms, and surface invariance is equivalent to the Gauduchon condition.",
    "Positive two-mass bounds and volume addition give a complete ordered diffuse model interval without assuming invariant mass; residual preservation distinguishes parameters.",
    "Disjoint residuals with equal scalar mass and anisotropic equal residual measures do not separate models; symmetric-difference box metrics prove compactness and complete fixed-product polytopes."
  ],
